# Web client

- The only UI: React 19, TypeScript (strict), Vite, Tailwind v4, Radix dialogs, TanStack Query, TanStack Router (code-based route tree in `src/router.tsx`, routes code-split with `lazyRouteComponent`), TanStack Virtual for log windowing.
- Wiring: `src/main.tsx` builds `AppServices` (`src/app/services.ts`: API client, endpoints, `SessionStore`, `SessionRefresher`, QueryClient) and renders `AppRoot`, which reacts to session events. Tests build the same services over a fake `Transport`; never add module-level singletons.
- API layer (`src/api`): `client.ts` is the only code that calls `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest`; every failure is an `ApiError` (`errors.ts`) parsed from the error envelope or mapped to `network_error`/`invalid_response`/`aborted`. Components show `describeError(err)` and the request id, never response text. All wire types come from `types.gen.ts` (generated; run `scripts/gen-types.sh`). Add routes to `endpoints.ts`, query keys to `query-keys.ts`, hooks to `queries.ts`.
- Session: token in memory, mirrored to sessionStorage (default) or localStorage ("Keep me signed in"), key `golem_deploy_session`; tabs sync over the `golem_deploy_session` BroadcastChannel. A 401 `unauthenticated` for the current token ends the session once (`SessionStore.handleUnauthenticated`). `SessionRefresher` refreshes 30 minutes before the JWT `exp` and on tab return after long inactivity. The displayed role comes from `GET /account`, never from the JWT.
- Credentials only as `Authorization: Bearer`; never cookies. Streams (job logs, server logs) use `client.stream` + `readLines` (`src/api/stream.ts`) through `useLogStream`; the contract has no resume offset, so streams are not auto-reconnected.
- React Query: no retry on 4xx, polling only for state that changes on its own or through other users and only while visible: job, project and server lists use `listPollInterval` (2 s while something is in progress, 10 s otherwise), plus job details, server detail, stats and status. Per-row actions own their mutation so pending state stays per row. Every job-returning action shows the job inline (`InlineJob`).
- UI primitives live in `src/components/ui`; reuse them instead of new class strings. Render server strings as text; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`; links from server data go through `safeHttpUrl`.
- The daemon serves the SPA with a strict CSP (`script-src 'self'`, `connect-src 'self'`, no external origins). Keep the build free of inline scripts, eval and remote assets.
- Contract mirrors: client validators (`src/lib/validators.ts`: repo URLs with the daemon's wording and `sameRepoOrigin` (an origin change requires new auth), env keys (only `GOLEM_` is reserved) with case-insensitive uniqueness, env values (single line, 32 KiB per variable counted in bytes, 128 variables / 64 KiB per set), static names, ports; `authTypesFor` offers no "none" for SSH URLs) and `jobPermissions` (`src/components/jobs.tsx`: restricted jobs, cancel by creator or admin) copy daemon rules for early feedback only; still handle the daemon's 4xx where the action happens. Change them together with the Go rule they mirror.
- Errors: `describeError` explains codes the daemon cannot (`bad_host`, `invalid_credentials`, ...) and otherwise shows the envelope message; `withWait` merges `Retry-After` into it as one sentence (a trailing "try again later" is replaced, not repeated). A 429 on opening a log stream is shown inside the `LogViewer` (`notice`) with a retry. Field errors go where the field is: `auth.*`/`auth` in project dialogs, `env.<KEY>`/`env_set.<KEY>` to the env editor row (`envErrorsFrom`), `servers[i].env.<KEY>` named in the import dialog, `image` on the image field. Job details are plain text and wrap in full.
- Servers pick images only from `GET /images` (by id); with none, explain that images come from builds instead of showing a form. Actions follow `state` (`status-badges.tsx`: `portLocked`, `serverActionsBlocked`): the port is locked while the container runs/restarts/is paused or the state is `unknown` (Docker unreachable for a server with a container), `unknown` disables start/stop/restart/delete and log reads, `missing` (no container) offers start but no restart or logs. `DELETE /servers/{id}` waits for its `server.delete` job: success is a toast, a failure is an error toast plus the job shown inline. Server logs arrive sanitised: the viewer dims escaped sequences (`\x1b[31m`, `\u202e`) and the job log's `container | ` prefix (`logSegments`) and never interprets them.
- Static files: the page shows `used_bytes`/`quota_bytes` (0: unlimited)/`file_count`/`max_files` and warns when queued files cannot fit (`fitWarning`). One file per request, so each row's status is its request's all-or-nothing outcome (5xx and network errors: "outcome unknown"); `createLimiter` in `client.ts` caps upload requests of the whole tab at `MAX_CONCURRENT_UPLOADS` (2, the daemon's per-account cap) and the page waits out a 429 from uploads elsewhere. `replace=1` only for files the user confirmed; a 409 offers Replace on that row; a 507 marks the file not stored.
- Mock layer: `src/mock/transport.ts` stands in for the Docker- and git-backed routes (projects, servers, images, static, volumes, their jobs) under `VITE_API_MOCK=1 npm run dev` (`GOLEM_DEPLOY_DEV_API` overrides the dev proxy target), following the final contract (access levels via the real `GET /account`, restricted jobs, follow cap, tail bounds, replace semantics, first-error import validation, SSH auth and origin rules, env field names, port lock, `unknown` state, image volume limit, static usage/quota/upload cap, escaped log output). `src/mock/transport.test.ts` pins those rules. Keep it in step with contract changes or delete the domain from it; it must never behave differently from the daemon. It is imported only behind `import.meta.env.DEV`; `vite.config.ts` refuses to build with the flag and fails if the mock reaches the bundle.
- Verification: `npm run lint` (zero warnings), `npm test` (Vitest + Testing Library, jsdom; `src/test/contract.test.tsx` and `src/test/contract-updates.test.tsx` pin contract behaviour), `npm run build` (type-check + bundle for Go embedding). For UI changes that touch the daemon, also run the built binary against a temp data dir (and a hand-labelled image for servers) and exercise the flow in a browser.
